From: Alan de Bortolo <info@designmastersolutions.com>
Subject: [PATCH] exclude_object: lift before the first travel after a skipped
 object

Applies on top of 0022-exclude-object-manual-move.patch (uses its
last_tx_position).

The slicer ends every object with a short ramped lift (OrcaSlicer: 0.4 mm of
Z over a few mm of XY) and then travels at that height. When the object was
skipped the head is not at the end of that object but at the end of the last
printed one, and the first move after the skip goes from there straight to the
end point of the ramp: the same 0.4 mm of Z is spread over the whole distance,
so the nozzle leaves the last printed part almost at print height and crosses
whatever is in between.

Seen on a real print file (abehrman's of 2026-11-01, Snapmaker/u1-klipper#22,
three objects skipped) run offline: 8 such travels cross finished parts with
less than 0.05 mm of clearance, with and without 0022.

_normal_move() now makes the Z part of that first move where the head is, then
the XY part. Nothing changes when the first move after the skip does not go
up, or when no object is skipped.

Regression test: tests/test_issue22travel.py in
github.com/DesignMaster-Solutions/u1-fixpack (red on stock and with 0022
alone, green with this patch).
---
--- a/klippy/extras/exclude_object.py
+++ b/klippy/extras/exclude_object.py
@@ -193,6 +193,7 @@
         # Ideally, there will be Z and E moves right away to adjust any offsets
         # before moving away from the last position.  Any remaining corrections
         # will be made on the firs XY move.
+        left_skipped_xy = False
         if (offset[0] != 0 or offset[1] != 0) and \
             (newpos[0] != self.last_position_excluded[0] or \
             newpos[1] != self.last_position_excluded[1]):
@@ -201,6 +202,7 @@
             offset[2] = 0
             offset[3] += self.extruder_adj
             self.extruder_adj = 0
+            left_skipped_xy = True
 
         if offset[2] != 0 and newpos[2] != self.last_position_excluded[2]:
             offset[2] = 0
@@ -213,6 +215,13 @@
         tx_pos = newpos[:]
         for i in range(4):
             tx_pos[i] = newpos[i] - offset[i]
+        if left_skipped_xy and self.last_tx_position is not None \
+                and tx_pos[2] > self.last_tx_position[2]:
+            # The slicer planned this lift from the skipped object's last
+            # point; the head is at the last printed one. Lift there first, so
+            # the travel does not ramp up across finished parts.
+            self.next_transform.move(
+                self.last_tx_position[:2] + tx_pos[2:], speed)
         self.next_transform.move(tx_pos, speed)
         self.last_tx_position = tx_pos[:3]
 
